export const DATA_49: ReadingTest[] = [
  { id: "rd-058", num: 58, title: "Đề 058", timeMin: 60, parts: [
    { title: "Understanding Relationships: Healthy vs. Unhealthy", paragraphs: ["Relationships are an important part of everyday life. Whether it is with family members, friends, classmates, or romantic partners, the way people connect with each other has a big impact on their happiness and well-being. Most people want to have good relationships, but not everyone knows what makes a relationship healthy or unhealthy. Learning to recognize the difference can help people make better choices and live more fulfilling lives.", "A healthy relationship is built on a foundation of trust and respect. In a healthy relationship, both people feel safe to express their thoughts and feelings without fear of being judged or criticized. They listen to each other carefully and try to understand each other's point of view, even when they disagree. For example, when two friends argue about a decision, they can talk about the problem calmly, find a compromise , and move on without holding grudges. This kind of open communication is one of the most important signs of a healthy relationship.", "Another key feature of healthy relationships is that both people support each other's goals and independence. This means that each person is allowed to have their own hobbies, friendships, and personal space. They do not try to control what the other person does or who they spend time with. Instead, they encourage each other to grow as individuals while also enjoying time together. This balance between togetherness and independence helps relationships stay strong over time.", "On the other hand, unhealthy relationships often involve behaviors that make one or both people feel bad about themselves. One common sign of an unhealthy relationship is when one person tries to control the other. This can happen in many ways — for example, by checking the other person's phone without permission, telling them who they can or cannot talk to, or making all the decisions without asking for input. These behaviors can make the other person feel trapped and powerless.", "Jealousy and dishonesty are also warning signs of an unhealthy relationship. While feeling a little jealous from time to time is normal, extreme jealousy can lead to possessive behavior. A person who is possessive may become angry when their partner or friend spends time with others, even in completely innocent situations. Dishonesty, such as lying about small things or hiding important information, can destroy the trust that holds a relationship together. Once trust is lost, it is very difficult to rebuild.", "It is also important to recognize that unhealthy relationships are not always easy to leave. People sometimes stay in bad relationships because they feel lonely, afraid, or because they still care about the other person. They may also worry about what others will think if they end the relationship. However, staying in a relationship that is harmful to your mental or emotional health is never a good idea. Reaching out to a trusted adult, counselor, or support group can provide helpful guidance and encourage people to make healthier choices.", "Ultimately, everyone deserves to be in relationships that make them feel valued, supported, and happy. By learning to identify the signs of both healthy and unhealthy relationships, people can protect themselves and build stronger connections with those around them. Education and open conversations about relationships should begin at an early age so that young people can develop the skills they need to form lasting and meaningful bonds throughout their lives."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main purpose of the passage?", options: ["To explain the psychological causes of unhealthy relationships", "To argue that all romantic relationships eventually become unhealthy", "To describe the differences between healthy and unhealthy relationships and why understanding them matters", "To provide a guide on how to leave a bad relationship"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what is described as one of the most important signs of a healthy relationship?", options: ["Making all major decisions together", "Sharing the same hobbies and interests", "Open communication where both people express themselves without fear", "Spending all free time together"], answer: 2 }, { q: "Which of the following is given as an example of controlling behavior in an unhealthy relationship?", options: ["Discussing disagreements calmly and reaching a compromise", "Telling someone who they are or are not allowed to talk to", "Asking a partner to share their feelings openly", "Encouraging a partner to spend time with friends"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what can extreme jealousy lead to?", options: ["Possessive behavior toward the other person", "Greater respect for each other's independence", "More honest communication about feelings", "A deeper sense of trust between partners"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, why do some people stay in unhealthy relationships?", options: ["They feel lonely, afraid, or still care about the other person", "They do not know what a healthy relationship looks like", "They are advised by counselors to remain in the relationship", "They enjoy the feeling of being controlled"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a feature of a healthy relationship?", options: ["Communicating openly without fear of judgment", "Having identical hobbies and always agreeing on decisions", "Trusting and respecting each other", "Supporting each other's independence and personal goals"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"They\" in paragraph 3 refers to _______.", options: ["Both people in a healthy relationship", "Counselors who help individuals with relationship problems", "Close family members who offer support", "People who are in unhealthy relationships"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"possessive\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Generous and giving toward others", "Feeling deeply sad about a situation", "Wanting to control or own another person", "Being honest even in difficult moments"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"compromise\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["A complete victory for one side in a disagreement", "A decision made by only one person in the relationship", "A refusal to discuss a problem further", "An agreement where both sides give up something to reach a solution"], answer: 3 }, { q: "What can be inferred from the last two paragraphs about the author's view on preventing unhealthy relationships?", options: ["The author suggests that education and awareness from an early age can help people build healthier connections", "The author thinks only adults are capable of recognizing unhealthy relationships", "The author implies that professional counselors are the only solution to relationship problems", "The author believes unhealthy relationships are impossible to avoid"], answer: 0 }] },
    { title: "The Hidden Dangers of Sugary Drinks", paragraphs: ["In recent decades, the consumption of sugary drinks has increased dramatically across the world. From soft drinks and energy drinks to flavored juices and sweetened teas, beverages loaded with added sugar have become a central part of modern diets, especially among young people. While many consumers are aware that too much sugar is not ideal, few fully appreciate the wide range of health risks that regular consumption of these drinks can pose .", "One of the most well-documented consequences of drinking too many sugary beverages is weight gain. When people consume more calories than their bodies can use, the excess energy is stored as fat. Sugary drinks are particularly problematic because they are high in calories yet do very little to make a person feel full. Research has shown that unlike solid food, liquid calories are not registered by the body in the same way, meaning that people who drink large amounts of sugary beverages tend to consume more overall calories throughout the day without realizing it. Over time, this pattern can lead to obesity, a condition that is strongly linked to a range of serious illnesses.", "Beyond weight gain, frequent consumption of sugary drinks is closely connected to the development of type 2 diabetes. When a person drinks a sugary beverage, the high amount of sugar enters the bloodstream rapidly, causing a sharp rise in blood glucose levels. The pancreas must then release large amounts of insulin to manage this sudden increase. If this process happens repeatedly over many years, the body's cells may gradually become less responsive to insulin — a condition known as insulin resistance. This is considered one of the primary pathways through which sugary drinks contribute to the onset of type 2 diabetes.", "The damage caused by sugary drinks is not limited to internal organs. Dental health is another area that is seriously affected. The sugar in these beverages serves as a food source for harmful bacteria living in the mouth. These bacteria feed on sugar and produce acid as a byproduct, which gradually wears away the protective enamel on the surface of the teeth. Once the enamel is damaged, the risk of tooth decay and cavities increases significantly. Regular brushing and dental visits can slow this process, but they cannot fully counteract the harmful effects of frequent sugar exposure.", "Heart health is also at risk. Several large-scale studies have found that people who consume one or more sugary drinks per day have a noticeably higher risk of developing cardiovascular disease compared to those who rarely drink them. The exact mechanisms are still being studied, but researchers believe that sugary drinks contribute to inflammation, high blood pressure, and unhealthy cholesterol levels — all of which are known risk factors for heart disease. Some scientists have argued that the cardiovascular risks associated with sugary drink consumption may be even more significant than those linked to dietary fat.", "Despite these well-established risks, sugary drinks remain extremely popular, largely due to effective marketing strategies and their widespread availability . Beverage companies spend billions of dollars each year advertising their products, often targeting children and teenagers with colorful packaging, celebrity endorsements, and sponsorships of major sports events. Critics argue that this aggressive marketing has made it socially acceptable — even desirable — to consume these products on a daily basis, normalizing habits that are fundamentally harmful to public health.", "Governments and health organizations around the world have responded with various strategies to reduce sugary drink consumption. Some countries have introduced taxes on sugar-sweetened beverages, while others have implemented restrictions on advertising sugary products to children. Public health campaigns have also played a role in raising awareness about the risks. However, experts stress that individual action alone is not enough — structural changes in the food and beverage industry are necessary to create environments where healthier choices are more accessible and affordable for everyone."], questions: [{ q: "What is the passage mainly about?", options: ["The various health risks associated with regularly consuming sugary drinks and efforts to address them", "The history of the soft drink industry and its global expansion", "A comparison of different types of sugary drinks and their calorie content", "The economic impact of sugar taxes on beverage companies"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, why are sugary drinks particularly linked to weight gain?", options: ["They contain artificial sweeteners that slow down metabolism", "They replace nutritious meals and reduce the intake of essential vitamins", "People who drink them tend to exercise less frequently", "The body does not process liquid calories the same way it does solid food, so people consume more overall"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what happens when a person repeatedly consumes large amounts of sugar over many years?", options: ["The pancreas stops producing insulin entirely", "The body gradually stops absorbing glucose from food", "The body's cells may become less responsive to insulin, increasing the risk of type 2 diabetes", "Blood glucose levels permanently remain low due to overproduction of insulin"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, how do sugary drinks damage dental health?", options: ["Frequent rinsing with sugary drinks washes away protective saliva", "The sugar directly dissolves the minerals in the teeth without any bacterial involvement", "Bacteria in the mouth consume the sugar and produce acid that erodes tooth enamel", "The cold temperature of the drinks causes tiny cracks in the tooth enamel"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following best describes the link between sugary drinks and cardiovascular disease?", options: ["Sugary drinks are believed to contribute to inflammation, high blood pressure, and unhealthy cholesterol, all of which increase heart disease risk", "Daily sugary drink consumption has no clear link to heart disease according to current research", "The cardiovascular risk from sugary drinks is far lower than that from dietary fat, according to all scientists", "Only people who are already obese face cardiovascular risks from sugary drinks"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a government strategy to reduce sugary drink consumption?", options: ["Running public health awareness campaigns", "Introducing taxes on sugar-sweetened beverages", "Banning the production of sugary drinks entirely", "Restricting advertising of sugary products to children"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"These\" in paragraph 4 refers to _______.", options: ["Harmful bacteria living in the mouth", "Dentists who study the effects of sugar on oral health", "The various sugary drinks described earlier in the passage", "The protective enamel layers on the surface of teeth"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"pose\" in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["To deny the existence of a particular problem", "To create or present a threat or risk", "To reduce or limit the impact of a danger", "To display or show something to an audience"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"availability\" in paragraph 6 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["The degree to which something can easily be obtained or accessed", "The high nutritional quality of a product", "The popularity of a product based on consumer reviews", "The financial cost required to purchase a product"], answer: 0 }, { q: "What can be inferred about the author's attitude toward beverage companies based on paragraphs 5 and 6?", options: ["The author is neutral and presents marketing strategies without any judgment", "The author believes companies should be entirely responsible for all individual health decisions", "The author is broadly supportive of companies that invest in sports sponsorships", "The author is critical, suggesting that aggressive marketing by companies has contributed to harmful public health outcomes"], answer: 3 }] },
    { title: "The Loneliness Epidemic Among Modern Youth", paragraphs: ["In an era defined by unprecedented connectivity, a paradox has emerged that researchers and public health officials are struggling to fully explain: young people, the most digitally connected generation in history, are reporting some of the highest levels of loneliness ever recorded. [1] Far from being a trivial emotional complaint, chronic loneliness is now classified by leading health organizations as a serious public health concern, with consequences that extend well beyond emotional discomfort into measurable physical and cognitive harm.", "Loneliness, as researchers distinguish it from simple solitude, is not merely the state of being alone. Rather, it is the distressing perception of a gap between the social connections one has and those one desires. This distinction matters considerably, because a person can feel profoundly lonely in a crowded room, at a busy school, or in the middle of an active social media feed. [2] Conversely, individuals who choose solitude and feel content with limited social interaction do not experience loneliness in the clinical sense. The subjective nature of this experience makes it particularly difficult to measure, yet surveys consistently show that adolescents and young adults report higher rates of loneliness than older age groups — a finding that has reversed the long-held assumption that loneliness is primarily a condition of old age.", "Several interrelated factors appear to account for the rise in youth loneliness. The restructuring of social environments is among the most frequently cited. [3] In many countries, extended family networks have weakened as geographic mobility has increased, leaving young people without the informal community structures that once provided a stable social backdrop. At the same time, competitive academic pressures and the rise of screen-based leisure have reduced the unstructured free time in which meaningful peer relationships have historically developed. Researchers note that it is precisely this kind of casual, low-stakes social interaction — sharing a meal, walking home together, participating in neighbourhood play — that lays the relational groundwork young people draw upon throughout their lives.", "The role of social media in this trend is contested and more complex than early public discourse suggested. Initial assumptions held that digital platforms were directly responsible for rising youth loneliness by replacing face-to-face contact with shallow online interaction. More recent longitudinal studies, however, have produced a more nuanced picture. Passive social media use — scrolling through content without actively engaging — does appear to be associated with increased feelings of social exclusion and inadequacy. Active use, by contrast, such as direct messaging, collaborative gaming, or participation in interest-based communities, appears to carry different and sometimes protective social effects. The distinction between these two modes of engagement is now considered essential to any serious analysis of technology's influence on adolescent well-being.", "The physiological consequences of sustained loneliness among young people are increasingly well-documented. Chronic loneliness has been shown to dysregulate the body's stress-response systems, leading to elevated levels of cortisol — the primary stress hormone — over extended periods. This prolonged hormonal disruption is associated with impaired immune function, disrupted sleep architecture, and a heightened inflammatory response. Neurological research suggests that the brains of chronically lonely adolescents process social threats differently, becoming hypervigilant to signs of rejection in ways that can reinforce and deepen the very isolation they experience. In other words, loneliness does not simply reflect a social deficit; it actively reshapes the neural and physiological systems through which young people interpret and navigate the world.", "Despite the growing body of evidence, institutional responses to youth loneliness have been slow and fragmented. Schools, which represent the primary social environment for most adolescents, have been identified as both a key site of the problem and a potential site of intervention. Some educational systems have begun integrating social-emotional learning curricula, peer mentorship programmes, and structured opportunities for cooperative activity into the school day. Critics, however, argue that these measures address symptoms rather than causes, and that without broader structural changes — including reductions in academic pressure, greater investment in community infrastructure, and more thoughtful regulation of digital platforms — meaningful progress will remain elusive.", "What is perhaps most striking about the loneliness epidemic among young people is the degree to which it has developed in plain sight, largely unrecognised until its effects became difficult to ignore. [4] The challenge ahead is not simply one of designing better interventions, but of fundamentally reconsidering the social architecture of modern youth — the environments, the rhythms, and the relationships that either nourish or erode a young person's sense of belonging in the world."], questions: [{ q: "Which of the following best describes the main argument of the passage?", options: ["Loneliness is a universal human experience that has always been equally prevalent across all age groups", "Social media platforms are the single most important cause of rising loneliness among young people", "Loneliness among young people is a complex, multi-causal public health problem with serious physiological consequences and an inadequate institutional response", "Young people today are lonelier than older generations primarily because they spend less time with their families"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what distinguishes loneliness from solitude?", options: ["Solitude is more harmful to mental health than loneliness because it is self-imposed", "Loneliness always requires physical isolation, while solitude can occur in social settings", "Loneliness is the distressing sense of a gap between desired and actual social connection, whereas solitude can be chosen and experienced contentedly", "Loneliness is exclusively experienced by adolescents, while solitude is more common among adults"], answer: 2 }, { q: "Based on paragraphs 3 and 4, which of the following most accurately represents the passage's view on the causes of youth loneliness?", options: ["Geographic mobility has no meaningful effect on youth loneliness because digital communication compensates fully", "Social media is unambiguously the primary driver, with structural social changes playing only a minor role", "The causes are multiple and interrelated, involving both structural shifts in social environments and the complex effects of digital technology", "Academic pressure alone is sufficient to explain the rise in reported loneliness among young people"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which type of social media use is associated with increased feelings of inadequacy?", options: ["Direct messaging with close friends", "Participation in interest-based online communities", "Collaborative gaming with known peers", "Passive scrolling through content without active engagement"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what neurological effect does chronic loneliness have on adolescents?", options: ["It reduces the production of cortisol, making young people less sensitive to stress", "It improves immune function by activating the body's natural defence mechanisms", "It accelerates cognitive development by forcing young people to become more self-reliant", "It causes the brain to become hypervigilant to social rejection, which can reinforce isolation"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as an intervention schools have already begun implementing?", options: ["Peer mentorship programmes", "Social-emotional learning curricula", "Mandatory reductions in the number of assessed examinations", "Structured opportunities for cooperative activity"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"it\" in paragraph 5 in the phrase \"it actively reshapes the neural and physiological systems\" refers to _______.", options: ["The hypervigilant response to signs of rejection", "The body's impaired immune function", "Chronic loneliness itself", "The elevated level of cortisol produced under stress"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"exclusion\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["A deliberate choice to withdraw from social obligations", "The feeling of being left out or not belonging to a group", "A medical condition caused by prolonged social isolation", "An official policy preventing access to digital platforms"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"dysregulate\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["To accelerate the recovery of a system following stress", "To disrupt the normal functioning or balance of a system", "To strengthen and stabilise a biological system through repeated activation", "To permanently disable a hormonal or neurological pathway"], answer: 1 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'This contradiction — being surrounded by connections yet feeling fundamentally unseen — lies at the heart of what many researchers now call a loneliness epidemic.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 0 }] },
    { title: "The Environmental Cost of the AI Revolution", paragraphs: ["The rapid proliferation of artificial intelligence technologies across virtually every sector of the global economy has been accompanied by a conspicuous silence regarding one of its most consequential dimensions: environmental impact. [1] While public discourse has devoted considerable attention to questions of algorithmic bias, job displacement, and data privacy, the energy consumption and carbon footprint associated with developing and deploying large-scale AI systems have received comparatively scant scrutiny — a disparity that arguably reflects the broader tendency to treat technological progress as inherently separate from ecological consequence.", "At the core of AI's environmental burden lies the computational infrastructure required to train and run modern machine learning models. Training a single large language model — a process that involves processing vast datasets across thousands of specialised processors over weeks or months — can consume energy equivalent to the lifetime carbon emissions of several passenger vehicles. [2] The carbon intensity of this process varies considerably depending on the energy sources powering the data centres involved: a model trained on renewable energy carries a substantially different environmental footprint than one trained in a region heavily reliant on coal-fired generation. This geographic and energetic variability means that aggregate estimates of AI's carbon cost, while alarming in scale, may understate the unevenness of the burden across different parts of the world.", "Water consumption represents a less frequently discussed but equally significant dimension of AI's environmental toll. Data centres require enormous quantities of water for cooling purposes — a necessity imposed by the extraordinary heat generated by high-density processor arrays operating continuously. [3] Some facilities consume millions of litres of water per day, a demand that places measurable pressure on local water supplies, particularly in arid or water-stressed regions where such infrastructure is increasingly being sited for economic and logistical reasons. The convergence of rising AI infrastructure demand and intensifying climate-induced water scarcity creates a feedback loop that has attracted growing concern among environmental researchers, though it has yet to penetrate mainstream discussions of AI governance.", "The hardware dimension of AI's environmental cost warrants equal consideration. The production of the specialised chips — graphics processing units and tensor processing units — that underpin modern AI requires the extraction of rare earth elements and other conflict-prone minerals, processes that entail significant ecological disruption in the regions where mining occurs. Furthermore, the rapid pace of hardware innovation in the AI industry generates substantial volumes of electronic waste, as previous generations of processors are rendered obsolete within years of deployment. [4] Unlike conventional computing hardware, whose product cycles have historically allowed for more gradual infrastructure renewal, AI-optimised chips face accelerated obsolescence driven by the competitive dynamics of the industry itself.", "Proponents of continued AI expansion frequently invoke the technology's potential to contribute to climate solutions — optimising energy grids, accelerating materials discovery for renewable energy systems, and improving the efficiency of agricultural and industrial processes. These applications are real and, in certain cases, demonstrably impactful. However, the argument that AI will ultimately pay its own environmental debt through future climate applications reflects a form of reasoning that critics have described as speculative offsetting: the present, concrete costs are balanced against projected, uncertain future benefits in ways that may never materialise at sufficient scale or speed to justify the upfront environmental expenditure.", "Regulatory frameworks governing the environmental dimensions of AI development remain nascent and largely voluntary. A small number of jurisdictions have begun requiring data centre operators to disclose energy and water usage, and some major technology companies have made high-profile commitments to carbon neutrality or net-zero emissions. The credibility of these commitments, however, is frequently undermined by accounting practices that rely heavily on carbon offsets whose real-world efficacy remains disputed. Absent binding international standards and independently verified reporting mechanisms, the gap between stated corporate environmental commitments and actual operational impacts is likely to persist.", "What distinguishes the environmental challenge posed by AI from many previous technological transitions is the speed at which its infrastructure demands are scaling. The time available to course-correct — to redesign the energetic and material foundations of AI development before its environmental costs become irreversible — may be considerably shorter than policymakers and the public currently appreciate. A technology that promises to accelerate human problem-solving capacity at unprecedented scale deserves, at minimum, an equally rigorous and transparent accounting of the costs its acceleration imposes on the planet it inhabits."], questions: [{ q: "Which of the following most accurately captures the central argument of the passage?", options: ["Public concern about AI has been disproportionately focused on environmental issues at the expense of more pressing social risks", "AI development should be halted until its full environmental impact can be quantified", "The environmental costs of AI — spanning energy, water, and hardware — are substantial and inadequately addressed by current discourse and regulation", "AI's contribution to solving climate change will inevitably outweigh the environmental costs its development incurs"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what factor most significantly determines the carbon intensity of training an AI model?", options: ["The number of researchers involved in the training process", "The duration of time over which the model is deployed after training", "The size of the dataset used to train the model", "The energy sources powering the data centres where training takes place"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, why is the siting of AI data centres in arid regions a particular environmental concern?", options: ["Regulatory oversight of environmental standards is weaker in arid regions than in temperate ones", "Extreme heat in arid regions makes processors less efficient and increases energy consumption", "Data centres in these regions are less likely to adopt renewable energy sources", "The water demands of data centre cooling systems place additional pressure on already water-stressed local supplies"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following best describes the hardware-related environmental costs of AI?", options: ["The primary concern is transportation emissions from shipping AI hardware across global supply chains", "AI hardware both requires ecologically disruptive mining of rare materials and generates accelerated electronic waste due to rapid obsolescence", "AI hardware production has a lower environmental impact than conventional computing because the chips are more energy efficient", "The environmental costs of AI hardware are offset by the fact that older chips can be fully recycled"], answer: 1 }, { q: "What is the author's view of the argument that AI will repay its environmental costs through future climate applications?", options: ["The author fully endorses this argument, citing several proven cases where AI has already delivered significant climate benefits", "The author does not address this argument, focusing exclusively on current regulatory failures", "The author regards this reasoning with scepticism, suggesting it relies on uncertain future benefits to justify present concrete costs", "The author views the argument as credible in principle but notes that its implementation requires better international coordination"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT described as a current measure addressing AI's environmental impact?", options: ["Corporate commitments to carbon neutrality or net-zero emissions", "Requirements for data centre operators to disclose energy and water usage", "The adoption of binding international standards with independently verified reporting", "The use of carbon offsets by major technology companies"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"whose\" in paragraph 4 in the phrase \"conventional computing hardware, whose product cycles have historically allowed for more gradual infrastructure renewal\" refers to _______.", options: ["Conventional computing hardware", "AI-optimised chips and tensor processing units", "The rare earth elements used in chip production", "The competitive dynamics of the technology industry"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"convergence\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["The gradual reduction of two competing forces over time", "The simultaneous intensification of two trends that together create a compounded problem", "The formal agreement between industry and government to address a shared challenge", "The divergence of environmental priorities across different regions of the world"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"speculative\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Relating to financial investment in emerging technology sectors", "Based on conjecture or uncertain projections rather than established evidence", "Based on thorough empirical research and verified outcomes", "Characteristic of reasoning that ignores the economic dimensions of a problem"], answer: 1 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'This selective attention has allowed the material and energetic foundations of AI to expand largely unchallenged by the scrutiny applied to its social and ethical dimensions.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 0 }] },
  ] },
  { id: "rd-102", num: 102, title: "Đề 102", timeMin: 60, parts: [
    { title: "Studying Abroad: Opportunities, Challenges, and Long-Term Benefits", paragraphs: ["Each year, millions of students leave their home countries to pursue education in foreign universities. Studying abroad has become one of the most popular and widely discussed options for young people seeking to improve their academic qualifications, expand their cultural horizons, and enhance their career prospects. While the experience offers many clear advantages, it also brings with it a set of practical and emotional challenges that students must be prepared to face before and during their time overseas.", "One of the most commonly cited reasons for studying abroad is the opportunity to receive a high-quality education at internationally recognized institutions. Many students from countries with limited access to specialized academic programs travel to destinations such as the United States, the United Kingdom, Australia, or Canada to pursue degrees in fields such as engineering, medicine, business, and the arts. Graduates from well-known universities in these countries often find that their qualifications are recognized and respected by employers across a wide range of industries, giving them a competitive advantage in the global job market.", "Beyond academic qualifications, studying abroad offers students the chance to develop important life skills that cannot easily be acquired in a classroom. Living independently in a foreign country requires students to manage their own finances, cook their own meals, navigate unfamiliar public transport systems, and resolve everyday problems without the immediate support of family. These experiences, while sometimes stressful, help students develop self-reliance, resilience, and problem-solving abilities that employers consistently rank among the most desirable qualities in a job applicant. Many former international students report that the challenges they faced living abroad were ultimately more valuable to their personal development than the formal academic content of their degree programs.", "Cultural immersion is another significant benefit of studying abroad. When students live and study in a foreign country, they are exposed on a daily basis to different social norms, communication styles, religious practices, and ways of thinking. This exposure can broaden their perspectives considerably, making them more adaptable and open-minded in both personal and professional contexts. Language development is an especially important aspect of this immersion: students who study in a country where a different language is spoken often make rapid and lasting improvements in their language skills, far exceeding what classroom study alone can achieve. This linguistic confidence, combined with cross-cultural understanding, is increasingly valued by multinational employers who operate across diverse markets.", "Despite these benefits, studying abroad is not without its difficulties. The financial cost of tuition fees, accommodation, living expenses, and international travel can be prohibitively high for many families, and scholarships or financial aid programs, while available, are highly competitive and do not cover all students' needs. Culture shock — the feeling of disorientation and anxiety that many students experience when adjusting to a new cultural environment — is another common challenge. Students may struggle initially with loneliness, homesickness, and the difficulty of forming meaningful friendships in an unfamiliar social context. Academic pressures can also be more intense than expected, particularly for students studying in a language that is not their mother tongue, who may find it challenging to keep up with lectures, participate in seminars, and complete written assignments to the required standard.", "Nevertheless, surveys of graduates who have studied abroad consistently indicate that the experience has a significant and lasting positive impact on both their careers and their sense of self. Many report that studying overseas helped them discover new interests, challenge assumptions they had held about themselves and others, and develop a more nuanced understanding of global issues. For students who approach the experience with an open mind and adequate preparation, studying abroad remains one of the most transformative educational choices available — one that can shape their personal and professional lives for many years to come."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of this passage?", options: ["The financial cost of studying abroad is the most serious obstacle preventing students from developing countries from pursuing overseas education", "Studying abroad offers both significant benefits and real challenges, and can have a lasting impact on students' personal and professional lives", "Students who study abroad consistently achieve better academic results than those who remain in their home countries", "International universities in English-speaking countries offer superior degree programs that are unavailable in developing nations"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what life skills do students develop when living independently abroad?", options: ["Advanced academic research methods, critical writing skills, and the ability to conduct independent laboratory experiments", "Self-reliance, resilience, and problem-solving abilities developed through managing daily life without family support", "Formal networking skills and professional etiquette required to succeed in competitive international job markets", "Time management techniques and study habits that help them maintain high academic performance under pressure"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"prohibitively\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Excessively, at a level so high that it prevents many people from being able to afford something", "Temporarily, in a way that creates short-term financial strain that diminishes after the first year", "Unexpectedly, in a way that surprises students who have not researched costs in advance", "Moderately, at a level that is manageable for most families with careful financial planning"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"their\" in paragraph 2 refers to _______.", options: ["The employers across a wide range of industries who seek candidates with overseas qualifications", "The students from countries with limited access to specialized programs who choose to study abroad", "The graduates from well-known universities in the United States, United Kingdom, Australia, or Canada", "The internationally recognized institutions where students from many countries pursue their degrees"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what is \"culture shock\"?", options: ["The social isolation caused by spending too much time studying rather than engaging with local communities", "The feeling of disorientation and anxiety that students experience when adjusting to a new cultural environment", "The academic difficulty experienced by students who must study in a language other than their mother tongue", "The financial pressure caused by unexpectedly high living costs in a foreign country during the first semester"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, why is linguistic confidence particularly valued by multinational employers?", options: ["Because multinational employers operate across diverse markets and need employees with cross-cultural understanding", "Because language skills are considered more important than technical qualifications in most international industries", "Because it reduces the need for companies to invest in language training programs for their newly hired staff", "Because employees who speak multiple languages can be paid lower salaries than monolingual workers in the same role"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a challenge faced by students studying abroad?", options: ["The high financial cost of tuition fees, accommodation, and living expenses", "Feelings of loneliness, homesickness, and difficulty forming friendships", "Disagreements with academic supervisors over research topics and methodology", "Academic pressure, especially for those studying in a language that is not their mother tongue"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"nuanced\" in paragraph 6 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Subtle and sophisticated, reflecting careful attention to complexity and fine distinctions", "Practical and applied, developed through direct professional experience in the workplace", "Formal and academic, based on theoretical frameworks learned through structured university study", "Broad and general, covering a wide range of topics without going into significant depth"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what do surveys of graduates who studied abroad consistently show?", options: ["That studying abroad has a significant and lasting positive impact on graduates' careers and sense of self", "That the academic qualifications gained abroad are valued more highly than interpersonal or cultural skills by employers", "That the long-term financial costs of studying abroad outweigh the career and personal benefits for most graduates", "That most graduates regret choosing to study abroad rather than completing their degrees in their home country"], answer: 0 }, { q: "What is the word \"This\" in paragraph 4 referring to?", options: ["The academic content of degree programs offered at universities in English-speaking countries", "The cross-cultural understanding that international students gain through living and studying overseas", "The linguistic confidence developed by students who study in a country where a different language is spoken", "The daily exposure to different social norms, communication styles, and religious practices in a foreign country"], answer: 2 }] },
    { title: "Operating Systems: The Invisible Architecture of Modern Computing", paragraphs: ["Every time a person turns on a smartphone, laptop, or desktop computer, an elaborate piece of software springs to life before any application is launched or any file is opened. This software — the operating system — is the foundational layer of code that manages a computer's hardware resources, provides essential services to application programs, and mediates all interactions between the user and the physical components of the machine. Without an operating system, the raw processing power of a modern computer would be entirely inaccessible: hardware and software would have no shared language through which to communicate, and the device would be unable to perform any useful function.", "The core responsibilities of an operating system can be organized into several broad categories. Process management involves controlling the execution of programs — determining which processes receive access to the central processing unit (CPU), for how long, and in what order. On a modern device running dozens of applications simultaneously, the operating system's scheduler must allocate CPU time across competing processes in a way that ensures each application receives sufficient resources while preventing any single process from monopolizing the system. Memory management is an equally critical function: the operating system tracks which areas of RAM are currently in use, allocates memory to new processes as needed, and retrieves it when processes terminate — a task that requires constant precision to avoid memory leaks or system crashes. File system management, device driver coordination, and security enforcement round out the primary responsibilities of any modern operating system.", "The history of operating systems mirrors the history of computing itself. Early computers in the 1940s and 1950s had no operating systems at all — each program was fed directly into the machine in binary code, and operators had to manually reconfigure the hardware between tasks. [1] The development of batch processing systems in the late 1950s represented a significant step forward, allowing multiple jobs to be queued and executed automatically without operator intervention between each one. [2] The concept of time-sharing, pioneered in the 1960s, was even more transformative: by rapidly switching CPU attention between multiple users' tasks, a single mainframe computer could appear to serve dozens of users simultaneously, dramatically increasing the efficiency of expensive computing infrastructure. [3] The introduction of personal computers in the late 1970s and early 1980s brought operating systems into the homes and offices of ordinary people for the first time, with systems such as MS-DOS establishing the command-line interface as the standard mode of human-computer interaction. [4]", "The graphical user interface (GUI) revolution, catalyzed by Apple's Macintosh in 1984 and subsequently adopted by Microsoft Windows, transformed the way ordinary users interacted with computers. By replacing text-based commands with visual icons, windows, and pointing devices, the GUI made computers far more intuitive and accessible to people without technical training. This democratization of computing is widely credited with accelerating the mass adoption of personal computers throughout the 1980s and 1990s and laying the groundwork for the internet era that followed. Today, the principles established by early GUI systems remain central to the design of operating systems on desktop computers, laptops, tablets, and smartphones alike.", "The landscape of modern operating systems is dominated by a small number of major platforms. Microsoft Windows retains the largest share of the desktop and laptop market, while Apple's macOS commands a smaller but highly loyal user base, particularly among creative professionals and developers. In the mobile space, Google's Android — built on the Linux kernel — accounts for the majority of global smartphone usage, with Apple's iOS occupying a significant minority share. Linux itself, available in numerous distributions and modifications, powers the overwhelming majority of the world's web servers, supercomputers, and cloud infrastructure, making it arguably the most consequential operating system in the world even though it remains largely invisible to most ordinary consumers.", "Security has become one of the most pressing concerns in operating system design. As computers have become ubiquitous and increasingly connected through the internet, operating systems have evolved into primary targets for malware, ransomware, and unauthorized access. Modern operating systems incorporate multiple layers of security mechanisms — including user authentication, encrypted file systems, sandboxing of applications, and kernel-level protection — to defend against an ever-expanding range of threats. However, the tension between security and usability remains a persistent design challenge: overly restrictive security measures frustrate users and reduce productivity, while permissive systems expose critical data and infrastructure to unacceptable risks.", "Looking ahead, the development of operating systems is being shaped by the growing demands of artificial intelligence workloads, edge computing, and the Internet of Things. As billions of low-power devices ranging from industrial sensors to household appliances require embedded operating systems capable of running efficiently on minimal hardware, the design principles that governed desktop systems for decades are being fundamentally rethought. The invisible architecture that makes modern computing possible is, in short, undergoing one of the most significant periods of reinvention in its history."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of this passage?", options: ["Linux is the most important operating system in the world because it powers the majority of global web servers and cloud infrastructure", "Operating systems are the essential software foundation of modern computers, with a rich history and ongoing evolution driven by new technological demands", "Microsoft Windows and Apple macOS are superior operating systems because they offer the most intuitive graphical user interfaces", "The primary challenge facing operating system developers today is creating software that can defend against increasingly sophisticated cybersecurity threats"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"scheduler\" in paragraph 2 refers to _______.", options: ["A user-facing application that allows people to organize their daily tasks and appointments on a digital calendar", "A component of the operating system that allocates CPU time across competing processes to ensure efficient resource use", "A security mechanism that prevents unauthorized applications from accessing restricted areas of system memory", "A hardware component inside the CPU that physically controls the speed at which calculations are performed"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what made time-sharing technology transformative in the 1960s?", options: ["It eliminated the need for human operators entirely, allowing computers to run continuously without any manual supervision", "It introduced the first graphical user interface, making computers accessible to people without specialized technical training", "It enabled personal computers to be manufactured at a low enough cost for ordinary households to purchase them", "It allowed a single mainframe computer to appear to serve many users simultaneously by rapidly switching CPU attention between tasks"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a core responsibility of an operating system?", options: ["Coordinating device drivers that allow the operating system to communicate with hardware peripherals", "Translating programming code written by developers into machine language readable by the computer's CPU", "Enforcing security policies that protect system resources from unauthorized access or malicious processes", "Managing memory by tracking RAM usage, allocating it to new processes, and retrieving it when processes end"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what effect did the graphical user interface have on computer adoption?", options: ["It replaced all text-based operating systems within five years of Apple's Macintosh launch in 1984", "It initially slowed the adoption of personal computers because early GUI systems required more powerful and expensive hardware", "It made computers more intuitive and accessible to non-technical users, accelerating the mass adoption of personal computers", "It primarily benefited professional programmers and developers by allowing them to write code more efficiently"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, why does the passage describe Linux as \"arguably the most consequential operating system in the world\"?", options: ["Because Linux introduced security features that have since been adopted by both Microsoft Windows and Apple macOS", "Because Linux has the largest number of individual user downloads and installations among all desktop operating systems globally", "Because Linux was the first open-source operating system to be adopted by major corporations for commercial use", "Because Linux powers the vast majority of web servers, supercomputers, and cloud infrastructure despite being largely invisible to consumers"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"This\" in paragraph 4 refers to _______.", options: ["The subsequent adoption of the graphical user interface by Microsoft Windows after Apple's Macintosh", "Apple's decision to launch the Macintosh computer with a graphical user interface in 1984", "The replacement of text-based commands with visual icons, windows, and pointing devices in early GUI systems", "The making of computers far more intuitive and accessible to non-technical users through the GUI"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"ubiquitous\" in paragraph 6 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Powerful and capable, equipped with processing speeds that far exceed those of earlier generations of computers", "Vulnerable and exposed, subject to a growing range of security threats from malicious actors on the internet", "Complex and specialized, requiring significant technical expertise to configure, maintain, and operate securely", "Present everywhere and in common use, appearing across a wide variety of contexts and environments"], answer: 3 }, { q: "What can be inferred from paragraph 6 about the relationship between security and usability in operating system design?", options: ["Users generally prefer maximum security over convenience, and operating system developers prioritize protection above all other design considerations", "The security mechanisms built into modern operating systems are so advanced that malware and ransomware no longer pose a significant threat", "Developers have largely resolved the conflict between security and usability through the introduction of biometric authentication", "Operating system designers must balance competing demands, as strong security measures can reduce usability while permissive systems create risks"], answer: 3 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'The introduction of personal computers in the late 1970s and early 1980s brought operating systems into the homes and offices of ordinary people for the first time, with systems such as MS-DOS establishing the command-line interface as the standard mode of human-computer interaction.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 3 }] },
    { title: "Crabs: Ecology, Behaviour, and Their Role in Marine Ecosystems", paragraphs: ["With over 6,700 known species distributed across marine, freshwater, and terrestrial environments, crabs represent one of the most ecologically successful and morphologically diverse groups within the crustacean subphylum. Found from the deepest ocean trenches to high-altitude mountain streams and tropical rainforest canopies, crabs have colonized an extraordinary range of habitats through a combination of anatomical adaptability, behavioural flexibility, and remarkable reproductive strategies. Their ecological significance extends far beyond their visibility as seafood: crabs occupy critical positions in food webs, regulate sediment chemistry, and contribute to the health of ecosystems from coral reefs to estuarine mudflats.", "The body plan of crabs — technically classified as brachyurans, meaning \"short-tailed\" crustaceans — is defined by the folding of the abdomen beneath the thorax, a feature that distinguishes true crabs from closely related groups such as hermit crabs and king crabs, which are not classified as true crabs despite the common use of the name. This anatomical arrangement, combined with the hardened exoskeleton known as the carapace, provides structural rigidity and protection against predators while simultaneously allowing the lateral movement that gives crabs their characteristic sideways gait. The carapace also varies enormously in shape and texture across species — from the smooth, hydrodynamic shells of swimming crabs to the heavily armoured, spine-covered surfaces of box crabs — reflecting the diverse ecological niches that different species inhabit.", "Crabs play a pivotal role in the cycling of nutrients within marine and estuarine ecosystems. As omnivorous scavengers , many species consume dead organic matter, broken shells, algae, and detritus, effectively breaking down complex organic compounds into simpler forms that can be absorbed and recycled by other organisms. [1] Burrowing species such as the fiddler crab are particularly important in intertidal zones, where their excavation activity aerates sediments, facilitates water drainage, and promotes the growth of salt marsh vegetation on which numerous other species depend. [2] In coral reef ecosystems, certain crab species act as cleaners, removing parasites and dead tissue from fish and other marine animals in mutually beneficial relationships known as cleaning symbioses. [3] Conversely, crabs are themselves a critical food source for a wide range of predators including octopuses, rays, seabirds, and larger fish, positioning them as important intermediate links in marine food chains. [4]", "The reproductive biology of crabs is both complex and fascinating. Most species practise internal fertilization, with males depositing sperm directly onto the female's eggs shortly after mating. Females of many species carry fertilized eggs attached to the underside of their folded abdomen — the same structure that distinguishes brachyurans — for weeks or months until hatching. The larvae that emerge, known as zoea, bear little physical resemblance to adult crabs: they are planktonic, transparent, and equipped with elongated spines thought to deter predation during their vulnerable early development. After passing through several larval stages, the zoea transforms into a megalopa — a transitional form that combines crab-like and shrimp-like features — before finally settling on the seabed and metamorphosing into a juvenile crab. This complex life cycle exposes developing crabs to significant mortality at each transition, and reproductive success in many species depends heavily on environmental conditions including water temperature, salinity, and the availability of suitable settlement substrates.", "Among the most studied behaviours of crabs is their system of chemical and visual communication, which plays a central role in competition, predator avoidance, and mate selection. Male fiddler crabs, for instance, wave their greatly enlarged major claw in species-specific rhythmic patterns to attract females and deter rival males — a behaviour that has attracted considerable interest from researchers studying the evolution of signal complexity and honest signalling in animal communication. Ghost crabs, which inhabit sandy beaches and dunes, produce stridulatory sounds by rubbing body parts together, a form of vibrational communication whose function in social contexts remains an active area of research. Chemical signals, or pheromones, are also widely used among crab species to communicate reproductive readiness, species identity, and territorial claims, particularly in turbid or low-visibility aquatic environments where visual signals are unreliable.", "Crabs face mounting pressures from habitat degradation, overfishing, ocean acidification, and rising sea temperatures associated with climate change. The dissolution of carbonate minerals in increasingly acidic seawater threatens the structural integrity of crab exoskeletons, which depend on calcium carbonate for their hardness and rigidity. Commercial species such as the Dungeness crab and the snow crab have already shown population fluctuations linked to shifting ocean temperatures, raising concerns among fisheries managers about the long-term sustainability of global crab harvests. Conservation efforts increasingly focus on the establishment of marine protected areas, the reduction of bycatch in trawl fisheries, and the monitoring of water chemistry in critical crab nursery habitats, though the effectiveness of these measures in the face of accelerating climate change remains uncertain.", "The ecological versatility of crabs — their capacity to thrive across habitats ranging from deep-sea hydrothermal vents to terrestrial rainforests — reflects millions of years of evolutionary innovation. As both indicators of ecosystem health and active agents of ecological change, crabs occupy a position of disproportionate importance relative to their size, and understanding the pressures they face is increasingly recognized as essential to the broader project of marine conservation."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of this passage?", options: ["Crabs are primarily important as a commercial seafood resource whose sustainable management is the central focus of marine biology", "The reproductive strategies of crabs are uniquely complex compared to those of all other crustaceans in marine environments", "Crabs are ecologically versatile animals that play critical roles in marine ecosystems and face growing threats from environmental change", "Crabs have evolved elaborate communication systems that make them the most socially sophisticated group of marine invertebrates"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"scavengers\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Filter feeders that extract microscopic nutrients from large volumes of passing water", "Parasitic organisms that obtain nutrition by living on or inside a living host organism", "Predatory hunters that actively pursue and kill living prey in order to obtain nutrition", "Organisms that feed on dead organic matter and waste materials produced by other living things"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, how do burrowing crabs such as fiddler crabs benefit intertidal ecosystems?", options: ["Their excavation activity aerates sediments, facilitates water drainage, and promotes salt marsh vegetation growth", "They compete with invasive species for territory, preventing non-native organisms from establishing in intertidal zones", "They filter harmful pollutants from sediment water, reducing toxic contamination in salt marsh habitats", "Their burrows provide shelter for juvenile fish and other small marine animals during low-tide periods"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT described as part of the typical crab life cycle?", options: ["A dormant pupal stage during which the developing crab undergoes internal organ formation underground", "A planktonic larval stage called zoea that bears little physical resemblance to an adult crab", "A transitional megalopa stage that combines features of both crabs and shrimps before settlement", "A period in which the female carries fertilized eggs attached to the underside of her abdomen"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what environmental factors influence reproductive success in many crab species?", options: ["Seasonal rainfall patterns, the abundance of detritus on the seabed, and the presence of cleaning symbiont species", "The availability of suitable mating partners, the depth of nesting burrows, and the density of competing species", "Water temperature, salinity, and the availability of appropriate settlement substrates for developing larvae", "The concentration of dissolved oxygen, the proximity of coral reef habitats, and the frequency of tidal cycles"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"stridulatory\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Generated through rapid movement of body appendages across water to create surface disturbances", "Emitted as low-frequency pulses through the water column to communicate over long distances", "Produced by the vibration of a specialized internal organ in response to external acoustic stimuli", "Created by the rubbing together of body parts to produce a rasping or scraping sound"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, why has the claw-waving behaviour of male fiddler crabs attracted research interest?", options: ["Because it offers insights into the evolution of signal complexity and honest signalling in animal communication", "Because it represents the most energetically costly display behaviour documented among marine invertebrates", "Because the patterns vary randomly between individuals, making it impossible to predict which males females will select", "Because researchers have observed that females consistently prefer males with smaller claws rather than larger ones"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"This\" in paragraph 2 refers to _______.", options: ["The hardened exoskeleton known as the carapace that covers and protects the crab's body from predators", "The enormous variation in carapace shape and texture observed across different true crab species", "The lateral movement pattern that gives crabs their characteristic sideways gait across sediment surfaces", "The folding of the abdomen beneath the thorax that defines true crabs and distinguishes them from related groups"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, how does ocean acidification threaten crabs?", options: ["It increases the concentration of toxins in seawater that accumulate in crab tissue and reduce their survival rates", "It dissolves the calcium carbonate that crab exoskeletons depend on, threatening their structural integrity", "It raises seawater temperatures to levels that disrupt crab reproduction and larval development processes", "It reduces the availability of prey species, forcing crabs to expend more energy searching for food sources"], answer: 1 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'Conversely, crabs are themselves a critical food source for a wide range of predators including octopuses, rays, seabirds, and larger fish, positioning them as important intermediate links in marine food chains.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 3 }] },
    { title: "The Origins of Human Language: Evidence, Theories, and Enduring Debates", paragraphs: ["Of all the questions that have occupied linguists, evolutionary biologists, anthropologists, and cognitive scientists, few are as simultaneously compelling and resistant to resolution as the origins of human language. Language — the capacity to produce and interpret an open-ended system of symbolic communication governed by grammatical rules — is among the most distinctive features of the human species, yet its emergence in the evolutionary record is extraordinarily difficult to trace. Unlike the bones and tools that populate the archaeological record, language leaves no direct physical evidence of its existence, compelling researchers to work instead from anatomical proxies , comparative primate studies, genetic data, and the indirect signatures that language may have left on prehistoric material culture. [1]", "The anatomical prerequisites for spoken language include a descended larynx, a vocal tract capable of producing the range of sounds required for articulate speech, fine motor control of the lips and tongue, and sufficient neural infrastructure — particularly in regions of the brain now known as Broca's area and Wernicke's area — to support the processing and production of complex linguistic structures. Fossil evidence suggests that the descended larynx, which is unique to humans among adult primates, was present in Homo sapiens by at least 100,000 years ago and possibly in earlier Homo species such as Homo heidelbergensis. The hyoid bone — a small, horseshoe-shaped structure in the throat that anchors the muscles involved in speech — has been recovered from both Neanderthal and early Homo sapiens remains in forms nearly identical to those of modern humans, leading some researchers to propose that Neanderthals may have possessed at least a rudimentary capacity for spoken communication. [2]", "Genetic evidence has added a further dimension to these debates. The discovery of the FOXP2 gene — sometimes informally described as the \"language gene,\" though researchers caution against this oversimplification — revealed that mutations in this gene cause severe impairments in both the production and comprehension of grammatically structured language in affected individuals. [3] Crucially, the human version of FOXP2 differs from that found in chimpanzees at two key amino acid positions, and these human-specific variants are estimated to have become fixed in the hominin lineage between 100,000 and 200,000 years ago. The Neanderthal genome, sequenced for the first time in 2010, was found to carry the same human-specific FOXP2 variants, a finding that has been cited as further evidence for some degree of linguistic capacity in Neanderthals — though most researchers caution that FOXP2 is only one component of a vastly more complex genetic architecture underlying human language.", "Perhaps the most contentious area of debate concerns the question of whether language emerged gradually through incremental evolutionary steps or appeared relatively rapidly as a consequence of a single key neurological or genetic innovation. The continuity hypothesis, associated with scholars such as Steven Pinker and Paul Bloom, argues that language evolved gradually through natural selection because it conferred direct fitness advantages — improved cooperation, more precise transmission of knowledge, and enhanced social coordination — on individuals who possessed even rudimentary communicative abilities. [4] The discontinuity hypothesis, by contrast, most prominently associated with Noam Chomsky and his collaborators, proposes that the critical innovation was not communicative ability per se but rather a specific cognitive operation called \"Merge\" — the capacity to recursively combine linguistic elements into hierarchically structured expressions of unlimited complexity. In Chomsky's account, this operation may have arisen as a by-product of evolutionary pressures unrelated to communication, such as the demands of spatial reasoning or numerical cognition, and was subsequently co-opted for linguistic purposes.", "The relationship between language and the emergence of symbolic thought more broadly has also generated significant scholarly attention. Some researchers have proposed that the explosion of symbolic behaviour documented in the archaeological record of the Upper Palaeolithic period — including cave paintings, personal ornaments, and engraved geometric patterns — represents indirect evidence of a fully modern language capacity, since the production and interpretation of symbols arguably requires the same cognitive infrastructure as language. Others, however, point to evidence of symbolic behaviour in earlier periods and among Neanderthals themselves to argue that the link between material symbolism and language cannot be assumed to be as straightforward as this argument implies. The ongoing excavation of sites in South Africa, Morocco, and the Levant continues to push back the earliest confirmed dates for symbolic behaviour, complicating any simple equation between symbolic culture and the emergence of language.", "Sign languages — the natural, fully grammatical languages that emerge spontaneously in deaf communities — offer a further body of evidence relevant to the evolution of language. The fact that signed languages exhibit all the structural properties associated with spoken languages, including hierarchical syntax, morphological complexity, and the capacity for displacement — the ability to refer to events and objects not present in the immediate context — suggests that language is not fundamentally dependent on the vocal-auditory channel. This finding has led some researchers to propose gestural theories of language origins, arguing that language may have originated in a system of manual gestures before being transferred to the vocal tract, possibly driven by the progressive freeing of the hands as bipedalism became established in hominin evolution.", "What the study of language origins ultimately reveals is the extraordinary difficulty of reconstructing a process that left no direct record and unfolded over timescales beyond the reach of documented history. The multiplicity of hypotheses — anatomical, genetic, archaeological, and cognitive — reflects not scholarly confusion but the genuine complexity of a phenomenon that sits at the intersection of biology, culture, and mind. Whether language is best understood as a biological instinct, a cultural invention, or an emergent property of increasingly complex social life remains, for now, a question whose definitive answer lies beyond the current boundaries of scientific knowledge."], questions: [{ q: "What is the primary purpose of this passage?", options: ["To argue that the continuity hypothesis provides the most scientifically credible account of how human language evolved over time", "To survey the multiple lines of evidence and competing theoretical frameworks used to investigate the origins of human language", "To demonstrate that genetic evidence from FOXP2 has settled the long-standing debate about Neanderthal linguistic capacity", "To compare the grammatical structures of spoken and signed languages in order to identify the original form of human communication"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what does the recovery of hyoid bones from Neanderthal remains suggest to some researchers?", options: ["That Neanderthals may have had at least a rudimentary capacity for spoken communication based on the bone's similarity to modern forms", "That the hyoid bone evolved independently in Neanderthals and modern humans through a process of convergent evolution", "That the anatomical prerequisites for language were fully established in hominins at least 500,000 years before modern humans appeared", "That Neanderthals possessed a fully developed language capacity equivalent to that of modern Homo sapiens"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"proxies\" in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Physical specimens preserved in the fossil record that provide direct confirmation of ancient biological traits", "Published academic frameworks that organize competing hypotheses about the emergence of human language", "Mathematical models constructed by researchers to simulate the probable development of early human cognition", "Indirect substitutes or indicators used to infer information that cannot be observed or measured directly"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what is the core argument of the continuity hypothesis?", options: ["Language is a by-product of cognitive capacities evolved for spatial reasoning and numerical cognition rather than communication itself", "Language evolved gradually through natural selection because even rudimentary communicative abilities provided direct fitness advantages", "Language emerged suddenly as a result of a single neurological mutation that gave early humans the capacity for recursive syntax", "The capacity for language was transferred from a gestural system to the vocal tract as bipedalism freed the hands for other tasks"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as an anatomical prerequisite for spoken language?", options: ["A descended larynx capable of producing the range of sounds required for articulate speech", "Neural infrastructure in brain regions such as Broca's area and Wernicke's area for processing language", "An enlarged prefrontal cortex associated with planning, working memory, and abstract reasoning", "Fine motor control of the lips and tongue needed for the precise articulation of speech sounds"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what argument is made by researchers who link Upper Palaeolithic symbolic behaviour to language?", options: ["Cave paintings and personal ornaments demonstrate that early humans had already developed writing systems before spoken language emerged", "The production and interpretation of symbols requires the same cognitive infrastructure as language, suggesting both emerged simultaneously", "Symbolic behaviour in the Upper Palaeolithic was exclusively produced by Homo sapiens, proving that Neanderthals lacked language capacity", "The geographical spread of symbolic artefacts across multiple continents confirms that language had become a universal human trait by this period"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what observation about sign languages has led some researchers to propose gestural theories of language origins?", options: ["Sign languages are processed in entirely different brain regions from spoken languages, suggesting they evolved through a separate pathway", "Sign languages lack certain grammatical features found in spoken languages, implying that gestural communication preceded full language capacity", "Sign languages exhibit all structural properties of spoken languages, suggesting language is not fundamentally dependent on the vocal channel", "Sign languages emerged more recently than spoken languages and therefore represent a later evolutionary development in human communication"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"co-opted\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Rejected and replaced by a superior cognitive mechanism better suited to the demands of linguistic processing", "Gradually refined and optimized through repeated use over successive generations of evolving hominin populations", "Deliberately selected and enhanced by natural selection specifically because of its advantages for linguistic communication", "Taken over and repurposed for a new function different from the original purpose for which it evolved"], answer: 3 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'This constraint has made the study of language origins one of the most methodologically challenging fields within the evolutionary sciences, requiring scholars to triangulate across multiple independent lines of indirect evidence.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 0 }, { q: "What is the author's overall attitude toward the current state of research into language origins?", options: ["Enthusiastic and partisan, clearly favouring the genetic and anatomical evidence over archaeological and cognitive approaches", "Pessimistic, arguing that the absence of direct fossil evidence means the origins of language will never be scientifically resolved", "Dismissive, suggesting that competing hypotheses reflect unproductive scholarly disagreement rather than genuine scientific progress", "Intellectually balanced, acknowledging the genuine complexity of the question while recognizing the value of multiple converging lines of evidence"], answer: 3 }] },
  ] },
];
